# Ecoma Identity — ignore rules.
#
# Two things here are load-bearing for security rather than tidiness, and both
# are checked by `tooling/scripts/check-architecture.mjs` (constraints §27 and
# §28: no production secret committed, no other Ecoma repository in this
# monorepo). Do not add an exception without an ADR.
#
#   * `.dev.vars` holds real development secrets and must never be tracked.
#     Only `.dev.vars.example` is committed.
#   * A `Cargo.toml` path dependency or a pnpm workspace glob reaching outside
#     this repository is a violation, not a convenience.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Rust
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
/target
**/*.rs.bk
*.pdb

# `worker-build`'s output, in BOTH places it can land. The `apps/**/worker/build/`
# form is what the tool wrote before the configs named `--out-dir`; the
# `.generated/cloudflare/**/build/` form is what it writes now, and is what
# wrangler's `main` points at. Neither is source, and `build/` is the name a
# bundler or a compiled artefact would use in any language, so this rule is
# written for the whole name rather than for the paths that happen to need it
# today.
**/build/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Node
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
node_modules/
dist/
coverage/
*.tsbuildinfo

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Nuxt
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.output
.nuxt
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# moon's task cache.
#
# `.moon/cache/` is generated state: task hashes and the workspace graph, both of
# which change on almost every run and both of which are a function of the
# machine that ran them. Tracking them produces merge conflicts that carry no
# information — two developers running different tasks get two different graphs
# — and it makes `git status` permanently dirty.
#
# The cache directory ships its own `CACHEDIR.TAG` precisely so tools skip it.
# `.moon/workspace.yml` and `.moon/toolchains.yml` are the two files that are
# NOT cache, and they are deliberately not covered by this rule.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.moon/cache/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Secrets. Nothing below may ever be tracked; the `.example` files are what gets
# committed, and they carry the shape of a value with no value in it.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
!.env.*.example
.dev.vars
.dev.vars.*
!.dev.vars.example
!.dev.vars.*.example
*.pem
*.key
*.p12
*.pfx
*.jks
secrets.toml

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Wrangler. Local D1 state, local KV and Queue emulation, and the auth token
# wrangler persists for `wrangler whoami`.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.wrangler/
.dev.vars.local

# The deployment topology is LOGICAL: it names Cloudflare resources and carries
# no generated identifier and no indirection for obtaining one. Resource ids are
# rediscovered from Cloudflare by exact logical name on every run and written to
# an ephemeral descriptor under the runner's temp directory, which is never
# uploaded and never stored in GitHub. There is consequently no `topology.local.json`
# overlay to keep untracked: the indirection it existed to hold has been deleted,
# and a local file that supplies ids would reintroduce exactly the second source
# of truth this repository removed.
#
# GENERATED CONFIGURATION IS NOT TRACKED. `.generated/cloudflare/**` is written by
# `pnpm infra:render` and consumed by `pnpm arch`, the architecture canary and
# `wrangler deploy --dry-run`. In `--stage resolve` those files contain REAL
# Cloudflare resource identifiers, so committing one would publish the id of the
# production identity database. The ignore rule is the backstop; the reason those
# files must never be committed is in `docs/security/secrets-management.md`.
.generated/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Test and coverage output
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.nyc_output/
playwright-report/
test-results/
screenshots/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Editor and OS
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
.idea/
*.swp

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The canary fixture's own git directory.
#
# `tooling/scripts/__fixtures__/violating-tree/` is a deliberately violating
# mini-repository, and building it sometimes leaves a real `.git` inside it (any
# command run there that shells out to git). When git meets a nested repository
# whose files are not already tracked, it refuses the whole path, reports
# `does not have a commit checked out`, and `fatal: adding files failed` — which
# blocks `git add -A` for the ENTIRE repository, so one stray fixture artefact
# could stop anyone staging anything at all. Ignoring the nested `.git` stops git
# treating the fixture as an embedded repository and removes that failure mode.
#
# THIS RULE COVERS ONLY THE NESTED `.git`. It is deliberately narrower than
# ignoring the whole fixture directory, because the fixture's files must stay
# tracked (see below). Measured boundary, A/B on a pristine repository with
# `git add -A --dry-run` as the probe:
#
#   fixture contents tracked,    no rule            exit 0
#   fixture contents tracked,    `.git` rule        exit 0   <-- today's state
#   fixture contents untracked,  no rule            exit 128 FAILS
#   fixture contents untracked,  `.git` rule        exit 128 still fails
#   fixture contents untracked,  whole-directory    exit 0
#
# So this rule is NOT sufficient on its own if the fixture's files were ever
# untracked — only a whole-directory pattern covers that case. The narrower rule
# is kept anyway, because it is the one that preserves the fixture, and the
# fixture is tracked in this repository and has never been untracked. If it ever
# is, `git add -A` will fail loudly and visibly, which is the honest outcome: the
# fix is to re-track the fixture, not to silence the check by ignoring it
# wholesale.
#
# This does NOT weaken the fixture's coverage. The fixture's own files are
# ALREADY tracked and stay that way — ignoring a nested `.git` cannot untrack
# anything. The §27 canary reads its `.env`, `tls.pem` and `.env.example`
# stand-ins from the git index, and stages them into a temporary index with
# `git add --intent-to-add --force` precisely because `.gitignore` correctly
# ignores those names for real files. Ignoring the fixture wholesale would make
# the canary vacuous while still reading as a green canary, so it is not done.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
tooling/scripts/__fixtures__/violating-tree/.git

# Agent worktrees: full checkouts of this repository, nested inside it, holding
# another session's in-flight work. `git add -A` would sweep a whole worktree
# into a commit, and prettier would rewrite the files inside it — both already
# guarded in `eslint.config.mjs` and `.prettierignore`, and this is the third.
# `.claude/settings.json` is deliberately NOT ignored: it is repository
# configuration a checkout should carry, not generated state.
.claude/worktrees/
